  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nShallow mid/upper level troughing will shift slowly east across the\nGreat Lakes region through the end of the week. As it does, several \nembedded impulses will move through the trough. This will lead to \noccasional chances for showers and thunderstorms over the next \nseveral days. \n\nThe first chance for precipitation will be Wednesday as an \ninstability gradient develops across eastern portions of our area. \nWhile forcing is limited, instability should be sufficient enough \nfor at least scattered thunderstorm development through the afternoon\nand lingering into the evening. Given the weak forcing and light \nflow, the severe threat will be low, but light flow/slow storm \nmovement will lead to the potential for locally heavy downpours. \n\nBetter chances for showers and thunderstorms will develop Thursday \ninto Friday and continuing into the weekend as somewhat stronger \nimpulses begin to move through the trough. This will also result in \nsomewhat better mid level wind fields/deeper layer shear. This would\nsupport the potential for a few stronger to possibly severe storms \nas we progress through the week.",
